From: OCCTSwift ecosystem <noreply@secondmouse.au>
Subject: [PATCH] Modeling Algorithms - BRepOffsetAPI_MiddlePath::Build carries a
 path that has reached a vertex forward instead of casting it to an edge, and
 stops where the sweep cannot reach the end section

BRepOffsetAPI_MiddlePath::Build starts one path per vertex of the start section along
the edges of the solid and builds one section per level from level i of two neighbouring
paths. A path that reaches the end section before the others is padded with its last
vertex, and a section at which both ends are vertices is already handled (the section
edge of the previous level, or the edge of the solid between the two vertices). What is
missing is the same handling for a vertex that is already a vertex, and a bound on a
sweep that never reaches the end section:

  - The pad casts the element at level i - 1 with the unchecked TopoDS::Edge. A path that
    was padded at the previous level ends in a vertex, so the pad casts a vertex, appends
    a null shape and the next myPaths(k)(i).ShapeType() dereferences it. The pad now
    appends the vertex again.
  - Where one end of a section is a vertex and the other an edge, with no connecting edge
    of the solid, the new edge needs the 2d point of both ends on the face. For a vertex
    the code took it from the edge before it in the path, cast unchecked, which is not
    there at level 1 or is itself a vertex. The point is now BRep_Tool::Parameters of the
    vertex on the face, the point the edge before it would have given.
  - No face holds the edge of a path and the edge of the section, so theFace is null. The
    search for a connecting edge of the solid no longer requires the edge to be on that
    face when there is none, and with no connecting edge Build returns.
  - GeomAPI_Interpolate::Load is given a tangent at every point, a zero vector where every
    path is a vertex. The flag array exists for this: the point has no tangent imposed.
  - BRepLib_MakeEdge on a null 2d line, or on vertices that are not on the line, faulted or
    threw. A null edge is now a refusal, and a candidate that is null is not "good".
  - The insertion of a vertex before level i (ChooseEdge == 1) casts the element at level
    i with the same unchecked cast; it now returns if the element is not an edge.
  - The level loop ends only when the section equals the end section, so a sweep that
    cannot reach it never ended: it ran until it faulted or, with a path carried forward,
    without end. A path holds each edge of the solid at most once, so the loop stops after
    as many levels as the solid has edges (the most any pair of the measurement below
    needed was a third of that). Build then returns with the builder not done, as it does
    when a start vertex has no edge outside the start wire.

Measured over 573 pairs of faces of 16 solids (Scripts/repro/3105-middlepath-patch), one
process per pair: 423 aborted on the shipped kernel and none with the patch, and none
runs on. Of the 196 pairs that share no vertex and are not the same face, 116 aborted; 81
of them now return a path and 35 return not done because the sweep does not reach the end
section. Every returned path was checked: a valid wire, connected, from the centre of the
start section to the centre of the end section, in the plane of each section, no shorter
than the chord between them and no longer than the longest guide path, and the same on
three runs; 14 of the 81 are marked doubtful because a spline leaves the bounding box of
the solid or loops. The 42 pairs that returned a path return the same one.

